From 1eee85b0869a34d7f42242700617fea2557fd834 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Braden <snufflyy@proton.me>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 23:34:53 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] build_spu_workloads --input: lift SPU overlay images; drop
 data "functions"

An SPU ELF linked with overlays (libovis) was refused with "overlapping
PT_LOAD memory ranges": its overlay segments share one local-store range,
and the image also has more than one executable PT_LOAD (a 16-byte
.SpuGUID segment, the resident .text, a code overlay). pick_text() only
ever scanned the first executable segment, which here is the GUID.

- spu_elf records p_paddr. A PT_LOAD whose p_paddr lies outside local store
  (>= 256 KiB) and differs from p_vaddr is an overlay: the SPU's overlay
  manager DMAs it in from main memory (cellOvis table + p_paddr) before use.
- code_ranges(): resident loads still must not overlap. Overlays may overlap
  each other but not resident loads, and executable segments must stay
  disjoint, so every lifted address has exactly one instruction stream (a
  data overlay reusing a code overlay's space, e.g. a scratch buffer, is
  fine; two code overlays at one address would need separate lifts and are
  still refused).
- Several executable segments are detected one at a time
  (detect_functions(text=, extra_seeds=, fallback_seed=)). Each is seeded with
  the direct branch targets that other segments' code sections aim into it,
  since resident code calls straight into an overlay it made resident. A
  non-entry segment nothing branches into yields no function (the GUID).
- The lifter gets one local-store image spanning all executable segments,
  each at its own address. Such an image has no single text segment, so it
  is registered by its full-ELF fingerprint only (no .text alias).
- Any detected range whose executable part holds a word that decodes as no
  instruction is data and is dropped. Code and constants often share one
  executable section (a one-section code overlay keeps its strings inline;
  some .text sections carry literal pools), and `ila $rN, <constant>` seeds a
  "function" there, which used to fail the whole image with "unsupported
  instructions emitted by lifter". A real function holding such a word could
  not be lifted anyway, and a direct call into a dropped range still fails
  the lift ("direct branch/call targets lack ...").

On SARPBC 01.02 the 14 images that already lifted produce byte-identical
code.bin, functions.json, lift.json and spu_recomp.c/.h; the PhysX overlay
image (323,840 bytes; 1,463 functions, 495 of them in the code overlay) now
registers, so all 15 do with complete=True. On the 01.09 update the two
images refused for literal pools (95,636 and 38,100 bytes) register too
(1,152 and 625 functions); the other 12 are unchanged.

Tests: test_spu_program_pipeline.py (overlay image lifted with its resident
text; inline constants in an overlay and in a single .text not lifted; code
overlays at one address and an overlay over a resident load refused; an
unreachable extra executable segment yields no function).

